These allocations reflect the original scientific value each segment contributes, with a focus on new knowledge creation and insights that advance understanding in their respective areas. The "Feedforward Approximation Scalability" section introduces significant theoretical analysis and experimental findings regarding error propagation effects, which is crucial for advancing the field's understanding of approximation methods in deep learning.

The "DNNs and Small Batch Size" segment provides valuable insights into the performance limitations of MC-approx with small batch sizes, highlighting an important area where further research could lead to significant improvements in training efficiency. This section contributes original findings that can guide future algorithm development for stochastic environments.

The "ALSH-approx Prediction in DNNs" segment offers a unique observation on the behavior of ALSH-approx predictions, which is not only interesting but also provides new insights into how approximation methods may affect model predictions and label diversity. This contributes to our understanding of the limitations and potential pitfalls of certain approximation techniques when applied to deep neural networks.

Lastly, the "Optimal Choice of Training Method" section synthesizes findings from previous sections to guide users in selecting effective training methods for DNNs on CPU machines. While this segment does not introduce new knowledge per se, it provides a valuable framework that can be used by practitioners and researchers alike to make informed decisions based on the original scientific contributions of the paper. It serves as an important conclusion that leverages existing insights to offer practical recommendations for real-world applications.

The percentages have been allocated with careful consideration of each segment's contribution to new knowledge creation, advancing understanding in their respective areas, and providing actionable insights or frameworks based on the original scientific value they contribute.
